.TH LUSTRE-CLUSTER 8 "2026-10-07" "lustre-clusterhat" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
lustre-cluster \- run a Lustre file system on a Raspberry Pi ClusterHAT
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lustre-cluster
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lustre-cluster
controls a complete small Lustre installation from the controller of a
ClusterHAT: a Raspberry Pi 4 carrying four Pi Zero 2 W nodes.
.PP
The four nodes are the Lustre servers.
They boot over USB from a root file system exported by the controller, and
their SD cards hold nothing but Lustre targets.
The clients are virtual machines on the controller.
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lb lb lb
l l l.
Node|Role|Targets at home
p1|MGS and MDS|MGS, MDT0000
p2|MDS|MDT0001
p3|OSS|OST0000
p4|OSS|OST0001
.TE
.PP
Both MDS nodes and both OSS nodes are active.
The nodes share no storage, so every target is a DRBD device mirrored
between the SD cards of its pair (p1 with p2, p3 with p4), and either node
of a pair can serve a target.
A target is
.I at home
on the node that serves it normally, and
.I active
on the node that serves it now.
.PP
All commands run on the controller.
.B lustre-cluster
re-executes itself with
.BR sudo (8)
when it is not started as root.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Setting up
.TP
.B init
Create the SSH key the controller uses to reach nodes and clients, and
install its public half in the node root template and the client image.
This runs once on the first boot of a freshly flashed controller, and is
safe to run again while no client is running.
.TP
.BR "nodes deploy " [ \fIN\fR ...]
Power the nodes off, replace each one's root file system with a copy of the
template, and power them on one at a time.
Without arguments all four nodes are deployed; this takes about half an
hour.
Lustre targets on the SD cards are not touched.
.TP
.B servers format
.B Destroy everything on the nodes' SD cards.
Partition them, set up the DRBD mirrors, and create the MGS, two MDTs and
two OSTs.
Needed once, before the first
.BR up ,
and afterwards only to start again with an empty file system.
.SS Starting and stopping
.TP
.B up
Power the nodes on, mount every target, start the client virtual machines
and mount the file system in them.
.TP
.B down
Stop the clients, unmount the targets and power the nodes off.
Run this before powering the controller off.
.TP
.BR "nodes on " [ \fIN\fR ...]
.TQ
.BR "nodes off " [ \fIN\fR ...]
Power nodes on, one at a time with a pause between them, or off.
A node that does not appear after being powered on is power cycled once.
.TP
.B nodes status
Show each node's power state, kernel and uptime.
.TP
.B servers start
Mount every target on the node where it is active, or on its home node if
it is active nowhere.
.TP
.BR "servers stop " [ poweroff ]
Unmount every target.
A node that does not finish within
.B UMOUNT_TIMEOUT
seconds is power cycled, or with
.B poweroff
left switched off.
.TP
.B servers status
Show, for each target, its home node, the node it is active on, whether it
is mounted, and the state of its mirror; then the memory in use on each
node.
A mirror state of
.B UpToDate/UpToDate Connected
means both copies are complete.
.BR Inconsistent " or " SyncTarget
means one copy is being resynchronised, which happens after a node was
powered off.
.TP
.BR "clients start " [ \fIN\fR ...]
.TQ
.BR "clients stop " [ \fIN\fR ...]
Start or shut down client virtual machines; all of them without arguments.
Every start gives a client a fresh root file system.
.TP
.BR "clients mount " [ \fIN\fR ...]
.TQ
.BR "clients umount " [ \fIN\fR ...]
Mount or unmount the Lustre file system in the clients.
.TP
.B clients status
List the clients, their addresses and their Lustre mounts.
.SS Failover
Nothing moves unless it is asked to: there is no cluster manager on the
nodes.
.TP
.BR "servers failover " \fIN\fR " " [ hard ]
Move every target that node
.I N
is serving to its partner.
The targets are unmounted on
.I N
and mounted on the partner.
If
.I N
does not release them within
.B UMOUNT_TIMEOUT
seconds, or with
.BR hard ,
it is powered off first, as if it had crashed, and powered back on
afterwards to rejoin its mirrors as the standby.
.TP
.B servers failback
Move every target back to its home node.
If a node will not release a target it is power cycled; while it is down
its partner keeps only the targets that are at home there, and once it is
back and resynchronised it mounts its own.
.PP
A move first waits, for up to
.B SYNC_TIMEOUT
seconds, until the partner holds complete copies, and refuses to do
anything if the partner cannot be reached.
Clients block while a target is moving and recover when it is mounted
again.
.SS Experiments
.TP
.BR "throttle on " [ \fIB\fR ]
Limit the disk I/O of the OSTs to a band of
.I B
MB/s (default
.BR THROTTLE_BAND ),
divided evenly between them: with two OSTs each may read
.IR B /2
and write
.IR B /2
MB/s.
This makes storage rather than the network the bottleneck, so the effect
of striping a file over several OSTs can be seen.
.TP
.B throttle off
Remove the limit.
.TP
.B throttle status
Show whether the limit is on and what is in force on each OSS node.
.PP
The limit is Lustre's own: the TBF policy of the network request scheduler
on the OSS I/O service, with one token bucket for reads and one for
writes.
While it is on, clients send 1 MB bulk requests, so one request is one
megabyte of streaming I/O; small requests cost a whole token each, so
small-file I/O is limited harder than the figure suggests.
The limit applies to an OSS node: a node serving both OSTs after a failover
gets the whole band.
It is applied again after targets or clients move.
.TP
.B benchmark disk
Measure sequential write and read speed of each node's SD card with direct
I/O on its swap partition; no target is touched.
.TP
.B benchmark net
Measure TCP throughput between a client and each node with
.BR iperf3 (1).
.TP
.B benchmark io
Measure Lustre write and read throughput with 1, 2, ... up to all clients,
each writing and then reading one file.
.TP
.B benchmark all
All three.
.PP
The amount of data per node or per client is set with
.B BENCH_MB
in the environment (default 256).
.SS Updating
.TP
.BR "update " [ \fIREF\fR ]
Install the
.B lustre-cluster
scripts, their systemd units and these manual pages from branch, tag or
commit
.I REF
of the repository
.B UPDATE_REPO
(default: branch
.BR UPDATE_REF ).
The previous scripts are kept in
.IR /opt/lustre-clusterhat.old .
A running cluster is not affected.
Lustre itself, the kernels, the node root template and the client image
are not changed: they are built into the controller image.
.TP
.B update status
Show the installed revision and the repository's.
.TP
.B update auto
Install
.B UPDATE_REF
only if it differs from what is installed.
This is what
.B lustre-clusterhat-update.timer
runs, a few minutes after boot and once a day, when it is enabled.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success.
Non-zero if a step failed or was refused; the message says which.
.B servers failover
and
.B servers failback
succeed when the targets end up where they were asked to go, whether or not
a node had to be powered off on the way.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/lustre-clusterhat.conf
Settings; see
.BR lustre-clusterhat.conf (5).
.TP
.I /etc/lustre-clusterhat-release
What the controller image was built from: Lustre and e2fsprogs versions,
kernel releases, date.
.TP
.I /opt/lustre-clusterhat/
The scripts behind the commands.
.TP
.I /var/lib/lustre-clusterhat/node-rootfs/
Template of the nodes' root file system.
.TP
.I /var/lib/lustre-clusterhat/vm/
Root image, kernel and initrd of the clients, their overlays, console logs
.RI ( client N .log)
and process IDs.
.TP
.I /var/lib/clusterctrl/nfs/p1 " ... " p4
The nodes' root file systems, exported over NFS.
.TP
.I /root/.ssh/lustre-clusterhat
The controller's key for nodes and clients.
.SH EXAMPLES
First use of a freshly flashed controller:
.PP
.RS
.nf
lustre-cluster nodes deploy
lustre-cluster servers format
lustre-cluster up
.fi
.RE
.PP
Log in to the first client and look at the file system:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo ssh \-i /root/.ssh/lustre-clusterhat root@172.19.180.101
lfs df \-h /mnt/lustre
.fi
.RE
.PP
Move the MGS and MDT0000 to p2, check, and bring them back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
lustre-cluster servers failover 1
lustre-cluster servers status
lustre-cluster servers failback
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what striping does when each OST is held to 4 MB/s:
.PP
.RS
.nf
lustre-cluster throttle on 8
# on a client:
lfs setstripe \-c 1 one; lfs setstripe \-c 2 \-S 1M two
dd if=/dev/zero of=one bs=1M count=96 oflag=direct
dd if=/dev/zero of=two bs=1M count=96 oflag=direct
lustre-cluster throttle off
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
.SS SD cards and USB boot
A Pi Zero's boot ROM tries the SD card first and falls back to USB boot
only if the card has a FAT partition without boot files, or if there is no
card.
A card with no FAT partition at all, exFAT included, hangs the node.
.B servers format
keeps a small empty FAT partition at the start of every card for this
reason; a new card must be formatted FAT, with no files on it, before its
node will boot.
.SS Moving the MGS and MDTs
Unmounting the MGS, and often an MDT, does not complete with the Lustre
version in the image: it waits for exports that are never released.
Moving these targets therefore goes through a power cycle of the node they
leave, and takes four to seven minutes.
OST moves are clean and take seconds.
.SS Memory
The nodes have 512 MB of RAM, about 460 MB of it usable.
A node carrying its partner's targets as well as its own runs close to
that.
.SS Throughput
All four nodes share one USB 2.0 bus, which carries about 25 MB/s in total
for client traffic and mirroring together.
Because every write is also sent to the partner node, a single client
writes at about 6 MB/s.
